<<~ ahu #/the-attractor>>

!! The attractor ~ STABLE FOUNDING

''STABLE FOUNDING'' names the state the house grows toward (operator-ruled). Once a Lararium stands, its
base state reads STABLE: a PRIVATE household SEED (its own charter, one seat) for the operator's own devices, and a FOUNDED PERSONA
ZERO. Everything else flows downstream of it — contracting into other Nexuses and founding a Nexus with a second operator, inviting the operator's own
PersonaGroup devices, and hosting guests who walk.

This carrier holds the road there and ONLY what still binds or stays unbuilt: the rulings (#/rulings), the
design decisions that still shape unbuilt work (#/decisions), the unbuilt slices in one order (#/the-road),
the fresh start (#/fresh-start), and the questions the operator still owns (#/open). Landed work reads in
git and the CHANGELOG, never here. The live wounds and the vitals read in
`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/er-handoff`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/rulings>>

!! Rulings ~ the operator's, as approved

!!! The live test and the fence
* ''The live test runs with TWO operators on two machines from its first day'': the operator and the operator's daughter, in one house. The face-switch act, the cabal plane, the custody seal and the engine fold ALL land before it.
* ''The fence.'' The second human's SEAT beside the first at the first Nexus's founding ENDS the alpha line. Every destroy-class or shape-changing act lands BEFORE it: the engine re-mint and the genesis re-bake; the carrier and key-class changes; the Stable re-found (AID, charter, seat, keyring); every signed-shape change; every load-bearing rename. After it, data deserves a migration path.
* ''A working Lararium comes first'': Live Wiki Browser -><- Live Node, under manual QA on one machine. Sensorium work waits behind that milestone.
* ''Face plurality stands as a day-one requirement.'' Each of the two humans wears at least three faces, each with a public Handle, on the first Nexus.

!!! The house as a container
* ''All CODE runs dockerized''; the host keeps only the hooks into the operator's environment (files and bags, session capture, the editor and CLI entry, custody).
* ''ONE Lararium per HUMAN, never per face.'' A human's faces keep their data together in one vessel (`PRIVATE-all, PUBLIC-one`); the recorded face-switch act carries plurality. No design assumes a container or a state mount per face.
* ''A SESSION wears the face; the vessel wears none'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-11; quote license: //"a human might want a tab open as one Persona, and a tab open as another Persona... same vessel. Identity as relation."// and //"a CLI session wears its own face too … those act as ephemeral UX"//). A vessel holds its roots and one store. A session — a browser tab, a CLI run — contracts in as ONE persona, and several sessions may live at once over one vessel, each wearing its own. Every act carries exactly one face. Two faces live from one vessel never link on the wire: each session proves itself under its own face-scoped key, never under a key the vessel shares across them. The vessel-wide parts (custody, the daemon island) serve every session and let none read another's planes.
* ''Local-first first.'' Cloud "backup larariums" under other humans' delegated keys stand DEFERRED until the operator can deploy to a cloud surface. Nothing in the container or the face-switch work may foreclose a keyless ciphertext carrier later.
* ''The boundary'' (settled by the house under those rulings): one multi-arch image; `/workspace` mounts bags and wikis, `/state` mounts vessel homes and custody, never an image layer; the local socket stays INSIDE the container and the host CLI enters through `docker compose exec`; the app never receives the docker socket; mDNS offers unauthoritative LAN candidates and a pinned admission alone grants authority; DNS-01 wildcard TLS supplies browser secure contexts; a release manifest binds the image digest to the genesis, engine and grammar CIDs.

!!! No global now
* ''A wall clock never decides whether an act runs.'' Abandonment, revocation and expiry ride causal events; a timer serves only its holder's own liveness, and a device clock may sit under an adversary's hand. Web2.5 timers (TLS acceptance, OAuth tokens) sit at the edge of the causal island.
* ''Lapse rides a causal fence'': an epoch roll, a burn marker, or causal supersession at a verified relation fence. A replica missing that history reports an incomplete view, never "expired"; quiet never means lapse.

!!! The Stable state and its consequences
* ''Every Lararium holds a charter.'' One operator's charter reads as a SEED and two operators' seats in one charter read as a NEXUS (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus">>). A household seed never grows into a Nexus; a Nexus founds fresh at threshold two of two. One "I" may hold several PersonaGroups. A Nexus grows by adding seats through its own rolls.
* ''Guests walk the household seed.'' Planes stay persona-scoped, so a guest sees its own documents plus the public and infrastructure shelf.
* ''Add-a-face (A).'' The daemon writes the public half; the terminal seals the recovery share; seating stays a separate act.
* ''Every leaf starts at the floor with its own root.'' A human may later JOIN a leaf to a PersonaGroup so its devices sync as one fleet; joining never takes the device's own root away.
* ''Founding persona zero belongs to standing'', never to a later verb. A capable vessel founds its household seed, so a "vessel with no charter" reads Mu.
* ''Stable folds into the re-found'' as its core.
* ''Forks report per board'': every presenter names every fork on the board it reads (the KERI watcher shape).

!!! Planes ~ cabal, realm, Nexus
* ''CABAL names the collective, REALM holds shared resources, NEXUS names the federated hardware mesh.'' The Nexus `@crossroads` board serves public-infrastructure docs and the Kahu Cabal keeps them. An operator's offering to a book club belongs in that club's realm, signed by the face worn there.
* ''Kahu drafts in the realm the charter derives''; publishing to `@crossroads` stands as a separate, recorded promotion act. A second Kahu realm gets made only for a genuinely different relation.
* ''Promotion approves the EXACT BYTES'' (hash, purpose, surface) under a threshold the charter sets, with a higher bar for charter, admission and trust-root changes. A CID, a clerk's edit or an active persona never stands in for collective approval.
* ''Steward signing.'' A scoped face signs acts that belong to one person; a transparent quorum Handle signs acts the collective owns; the root never appears publicly. A human wears MANY faces and many public Handles, each in many cabals, and signs a steward edge with the face or Handle worn IN THAT CABAL.
* ''Oracle answer'': one public answer per Nexus, signed by that Nexus's Kahu steward; a herm carries and presents it and signs nothing public. The engine floor rides as one shared content-addressed bake, and each answer names its floor CID (operator ruling).
* ''Herm tending'': tending may reach the herm's key; the house accepts that reach and names it (operator ruling).
* ''Introduction and admission'': a user introduces; an operator admits; the answer consumes the introduction (operator ruling).
* ''Names and counters'': the keyring epoch name joins (Nexus AID, `epochCid`); a counter orders one writer's own emissions only. The convergence secret stays per Nexus, and the face lives at the session capability (operator ruling).
* ''Kahu acts fall into four chartered classes'': ordinary (1-of-1); high-impact — charter, admission, trust root (3-of-5, or 2-of-3 for a small body); emergency (2-of-3, a narrow pre-authorized power reviewed by a later causal act); revocation (the threshold of the original act). A challenge appends to the record and may pause LOCAL reliance; it never halts the protocol. Admission of a Kahu or a Lamplighter always takes the seat quorum, and no ordinary single-seat class covers it; kapae and rolls take the quorum too; the user road rides its own bound (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus">>).
* ''Renewal runs as three sequences, kept apart'' (the realm feed, a per-renewal causal position, the KEL), and lapse arrives as three distinct events: signed departure, recorded removal by relation-scoped kapae, renewal supersession.
* ''A cancel crossing a herm yields three receipts'': the caller's abandonment, the relay's delivery (transit only), the vessel's outcome. A caller timeout never counts as a cancel; eviction answers `retention-gap`.
* ''One face at a time names WHO ANSWERS for an act.'' The switch law reads in `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-policy#/one-face`.
* ''A session wears the face; the vessel wears none'' (operator ruling).

!!! Custody ~ the Vessel KEK
* One Vessel KEK (VK), minted at the Stable founding, lives only inside LUKS-style keyslots, each a Clevis `sss` tree. Pin kinds: passphrase · paper (SLIP-39) · ember · tpm (one share only).
* ''EMBER'' names the Tang-shaped, per-relation device pin; it holds no share of VK. Only the blinded points cross on unlock.
* ''A backup route stands at every tier.'' Every keyslot tree, household or civic, keeps a human route (passphrase or paper); bind refuses a tree without one. Embers never stand as the only way in.
* ''The floor key keeps the vessel's public name''; the sealed custody root derives the veil and the sovereign caps. The veil change stays destroy-class.
* ''Unbind offers a VK rotation loudly'', every time, never folded in silently.
* ''Locality law'': an unattended ember answers on the local link only; a relay pin wants an attending hand. The vessel rests at the floor and raises when t embers answer; the raise lapses by non-renewal.
* ''HOT ⊥ COLD'': VK and keyhive state stay hot; the root, the recovery shares and the next-root seed unwrap per act.
* Measuring hardware x-only ECDH (phone enclaves) before trusting an enclave-held ember: approved, deferred.

!!! The hosted walk (F1)
* ''(c′) adopted.'' The HOST's gate reads and burns invites. The gate sorts every socket into same-operator · contracted · walker · stranger; under PRIVATE a stranger meets silence before any session frame. The guest carries its hosting grant; the hearth stores nothing about it. Invites mint by blind POPRF evaluation, so who-invited-whom stays non-computable. Revocation runs by kapae of the guest's leaf, by non-renewal, or by an epoch roll. Sybils meet tolerance, never detection.
* ''The inviter's face never signs an invite'': that signature would link the inviter at redemption. The hearth's blind evaluation carries the proof instead.
* ''V — vesting by class.'' A guest the HOST invites holds floor 1 at once; a guest a WALKER invites vests at the host's next roll. The roll reads as the household's consent to grow, and purpose labels separate the two classes without naming the minter.
* ''W — the hearth carries a walker's own documents'' as sealed ciphertext (carry ⊥ read), under a quota per guest. The PersonaGroup holds the durable copy; hearth carriage stays a convenience, never a backup.
* ''W reclaim.'' Quota pressure alone triggers it; lapse reads as a RATIO against the guest's own rhythm between contacts, so the host's roll cadence cancels; the most-lapsed-for-itself goes first; notice on the guest's next contact comes before any discard. The ruling sets aside counting the host's rolls: it measures the host's diligence, and diligence confers no title.
* ''N — a node may walk.'' The sorter sorts by proven relation, never by device type.

!!! The cleanup of the hosted walk (B1)
* ''The worn face hosts and walks.'' No roster order picks anything; a vessel wearing no face hosts and walks nowhere. Before its first hosting act in a Nexus, a roll refuses when the hosting doc holds an act this face signed that no local state holds.
* ''A herm answers the knock.'' A charterless herm answers any proven peer that knocked (`ownPlacePosture`): a waystone by class, never a broken lararium. A lararium reads its charter, and an absent or torn charter reads PRIVATE.
* ''A boot follows `LAR_ROOT`.'' A vessel isolated under `LAR_ROOT` projects into that root, never into the tracked tree; the mirror roots never read `LAR_BAGS`, so the isolation instruction names `LAR_ROOT`.
* ''Compartments outrank admission control.'' A walker reads its own hearth's hosting doc, never the carriage board: the carrier set stays a contract ledger read by those who hold contracts.

!!! The fresh start
* ''No pave or wipe command'' — such a command carries too much risk. See #/fresh-start.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/decisions>>

!! Decisions ~ what still shapes unbuilt work, and why

The hosted walk's own mechanics (the token, the grant, the spent-set, the hosting doc) read in
`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-invite`. The decisions below constrain a slice
still on the road.

* ''One sorter, inside the gate, before the verdict.'' Every gate instance (`/ws`, `/oracle`, the membership relay) sorts before `lar:auth-ok`, so posture reads once, at admission, and never again at share time. Two readers of one posture drift. R-WIRE keeps it: one gate per (Nexus, route).
* ''The knock.'' A gate answers an upgrade only on a path derived from its own key (`gate-knock`), so a dialer holding no pin draws no HTTP 101 — the WireGuard mac1 shape. The path still rides the upgrade in the clear on plain `ws`; full client-proves-first waits for Noise IK (R-WIRE).
* ''Silence, one deadline.'' No denial frame and no close codes: one drop deadline drawn at accept, so verify time leaks nothing and a PRIVATE stranger looks exactly like a failed proof.
* ''The multi-Nexus fold.'' A gate answers strangers if ANY Nexus it stands in reads OPEN. One gate key serves every Nexus a vessel carries, so this rule reduces exactly to per-Nexus posture once each Nexus carries its own wire key (R-WIRE) — not an interim.
* ''Carriage keys on the guest's PROVEN leaf.'' A hearth-issued stub would read as a bearer capability to another guest's record and as a hearth-minted cross-lineage linker. The leaf already proves on every socket, so keying on it adds no linkage the hearth lacks; a fresh leaf opens a fresh carriage (#/open, carriage across leaves).
* ''One store door.'' A command that touches the store runs inside the standing vessel over the owner-only socket, else owns the store itself for that moment. A second Repo beside a live holder writes bytes the replica never sees. `tools/direct-repo-witness.sh` names the two lawful `new Repo(` sites and reads any other as DRIFT.
* ''No hearth-local deny set.'' Kapae and the roll suffice; a deny set would make a seizable list.
* ''No node carriage.'' A node serves as its own durable store and no story walks its carriage; ruling N holds as a sorter property and W as the hearth's service. Node carry would open a new user story, never owed by N or W.
* ''Hosting stays on the worn persona leaf.'' ROOT-ON-FOUNDER keeps a persona root on one vessel, so one face cannot host from two stores unseen; the first-roll rail surfaces the remaining case. The face-switch act inherits it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-road>>

!! The road ~ what stays unbuilt, in the order a builder walks it

''The organizing law'': identity bytes (the Nexus AID, the device edge, the dyad scope, the seats, the
sentinel) change ONCE, inside one train. ''The owner law'': each slice lands its logic in modules, and one
named owner per step commits the one compose hunk in `open-node-vessel.ts` and `open-browser-vessel.ts`;
every other lane stops at a module boundary. Each lane's first commit carries its canon. ''The fence''
(#/rulings) bounds the whole road: every slice that changes a shape lands before the second operator seats a key in the first Nexus.

''The order'': GROUND → SUBSTRATE → HARBOR → KAHU PLANE → ONE FACE → TWO HEARTHS. The re-found's train
rides inside it — R-ENGINE's fold and R-CUSTODY's seal in SUBSTRATE; R-CHARTER → R-WIRE, then R-KAHU,
R-ENGINE's offers and R-EMBER, in the KAHU PLANE. Where the train's older order (the engine after the wire)
and the six paths disagree, the operator's rulings of 2026-10-11 decide: the engine fold and the custody
seal land early, and the container's tail lands before the charter train.

!!! 1 · GROUND ~ the residue (no identity bytes; waits on nothing; rides beside SUBSTRATE on disjoint files)
* ''Outcome-doc rotation'', so the receipt ring bounds bytes as well as count.
* ''The herm route for cancel and receipts'': the three receipts of #/rulings, mesh → node → CLI.
* ''`KeyhiveIdentitySlot` production wiring.''
* ''The evergreen sweep and the quiet doors'', itemized in `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/er-handoff#/wounds`.
* ''Measures'': the full-corpus per-verb flush re-measure; pack-PIN recursion; a production `assertBodyIndexTier` caller.
* ''Wiki shore echo law'': an inbound apply writes nothing back. A title named once can raise several changes; every leaked change routes as a save (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/outbound-bridge`).
* ''Canon direction the build owes'': canon crosses hearth → leaf; a leaf never writes the hearth's canon documents, and edits land in the working layer.
* ''Clockless shore law the build owes'': no wall clock decides; replace the echo-set grace and readerless `minted-on` stamps (`packages/lararium-tw5/src/island-adaptor.ts:274-292`; `packages/lararium-keyhive/src/resolve-binding.ts:158`).

!!! 2 · SUBSTRATE ~ two SOLO relays, the fold first, then the seal
Each runs one spirit per commit, and every commit leaves HEAD building. No other node lane runs beside the
seal: it owns the compose hunk.

''R-ENGINE, the fold.''
* The R-ENGINE fold target dependency line, in fixed order: automerge-repo and its four listed adapters at alpha.5 → `@automerge/automerge` 3.4.1 → `@keyhive/keyhive` 0.3.0-alpha.1; no Subduction or Onomancy dependency → the genesis re-bake (`GENESIS_ENGINE_CID`, every fixture holding the hearth true-name, the untracked CAS in the same commit) → the fold's own live proof on the rebuilt image, with a pushed shard run reading green. Current manifests pin automerge-repo and its listed adapters at 2.6.0-alpha.3, `@automerge/automerge` at 3.4.1 (`packages/lararium-node/package.json:25-30`), and `@keyhive/keyhive` at 0.1.0-alpha.8 (`packages/lararium-keyhive/package.json:26`).
* TW5 core and the memetic-wikitext bootstrap fold into `engineCid`; the vocabulary leaves the engine as content; the coal-only seed carries no `schemaVersion`; the island `schema_version` drops its counter.

''R-CUSTODY, the seal'' (P3-SEAL).
* ''What it owes'': every `hot` and `cold` row of the carrier table (`vault-carriers.ts`) except the custody root still rests outside the VK — its writer's bytes pass through the one sealed writer (sidecar → re-open → compare → rename, never sealing in place) and its reader through the sealed open. One commit per writer family, derived from the table's writer column, never from a hand list.
* ''The keyring law the build owes'': name an epoch by (Nexus AID, `epochCid`); let a counter order one writer's own emissions only; keep the convergence secret per Nexus and the face at the session capability. Mint only at the seat. Join delivery by scoped key and refuse different bytes under one name. Keep every verified fork head visible; retain by causal evidence; pass hot bytes through the sealed writer (operator ruling).
* ''The Nexus keyring debt'': the store keys entries by numeric epoch, mints at boot, overwrites a same-epoch delivery, and writes clear JSON (`packages/lararium-node/src/nexus-convergence-secret-store.ts:35-60,72-81,100-116`).
* ''First commit, the session and the boot'': the daemon stands at the floor holding no VK and raises on a socket verb whose credential the CLI reads on its TTY; until then `floorWriteCure`'s advice (`lares vault unlock`) names a door that does not yet cure. The test door's marker earns one loud line in `vault status` and the boot log.
* ''Facts to plan around'': the hosting spend log APPENDS, and a whole-file sealed writer cannot, so it moves to one sealed file per spend; the CLI-side writers move behind node functions, so no CLI file writes a custody row.
* ''Last commit, the retirement'': `vault seal|rotate|export|repair|passphrase` and `LARES_ARCHIVE_PASSPHRASE` (with its `_NEW` twin in `tools/contract-witness.sh`) leave with the passphrase lifecycle and the cleartext archive mode — never sooner, since they hold the only seal four carriers carry; the bare-row rotation preflight leaves, and the rotation's own census runs BEFORE the unbind, so a carrier that refuses the rotation leaves the slot bound; `vault unbind --rotate` then completes on a real founded vessel, where it refuses by name until that day.
* The census DERIVES from the carrier table, and a witness refuses any 0600 file or seal outside it. The enumerable carriage rows and the leaf-testability bound close at rest here.

!!! 3 · HARBOR ~ the container's tail
* ''Canon for the boundary'': the container rulings of #/rulings hold no carrier of their own yet; the lane's first commit writes it.
* ''A persona door before `up`'': the shim founds, then stops at `persona` until the service stands; a face lights only after `up` plus a restart.
* ''A fresh container vessel projects into `/workspace`'', and a fresh founding serves a wiki whose recipe resolves: today the workspace stays empty and a bare `meme get` reads `not-found` where `--bag` reads the carrier.
* ''LAN candidate discovery'': two machines find candidate URLs and keys on a household LAN; a wrong key refuses, a right key still wants explicit admission; the QR or URI fallback covers a LAN multicast cannot cross.
* ''Household HTTPS'': a DNS-01 wildcard on a domain the Kahu holds, one split-horizon resolver on the always-on node, and no install on any device (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/secure-context`).
* ''The harnesses stand on the image'': the mesh, kahu, crossing, swarm and burn compose files and their witnesses run the built image by digest and regain no source bind.
* ''The release manifest'': a multi-arch index whose record binds the image digest to the genesis, engine and grammar CIDs. Waits on the fold for its final CIDs.

!!! 4 · KAHU PLANE ~ the realms, the signer, and the one AID train
Build order: the realm and signer shapes against the rulings; then R-CHARTER, the one train that moves
identity bytes; then R-WIRE, so every pin changes once.

* ''Realms'': many cabal realms per Nexus with selected-realm routing, where one charter-derived realm plane stands today. A cabal reads as its relation closure and takes no key or roster of its own.
* ''The steward signer'': the worn face or a quorum Handle, never the root. The docker `meme` scenario fails at "B edits, promotes" — the steward gets named by a per-Nexus LEAF key while the realm-bag co-sign looks for the persona ROOT — and it proves the end-state signer, never a root workaround and never a leaf fallback.
* ''Thresholded admission and promotion'': the four chartered classes and exact-bytes promotion of #/rulings; chartered Kahu-cabal admission as its own authority act, where a vouch alone admits nobody.

''R-CHARTER, the one AID train'' (waits on SUBSTRATE whole).
* ''The Stable founding act'': one interactive act under one passphrase (the VK's first slot) yields the place, persona zero, a PRIVATE seed charter (one seat, t=1, the seat's own key and its cold next digest) and a keyring named by (Nexus AID, `epochCid`), with a commitment to the secret inside the seat-authored epoch act; mint only at the seat, never at boot (operator ruling).
* ''The Nexus founding act'': two founders, each from their own vault, seat one key each in a fresh joint genesis at two of two; each reads the other's seat fingerprint aloud; each seat commits its own cold next digest; each founder then signs a carriage contract that both seats countersign (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus">>).
* ''Charter shape'': per-seat pre-rotation (`nextKeyDigests`, `nextThreshold`) replacing the single next-key commit; the cadence inception cited once by the founding act; the seal-roll successor takes the name `charter-seat-roll`; `epoch0-<sha>` names become `nexus-<sha>`; `genesisSealEpochCid` deleted; the seat arms its cold next key at Stable founding (operator ruling).
* ''Re-scoped keys'': the chairs re-key to their own SEAT keys, apart from persona roots and per-Nexus dialing leaves (a root-signing seat breaches Q1-strict; a leaf-signing seat puts a warm dialing key in the quorum); the device edge and the dyad scope commit to the Nexus AID; the sentinel (the MeshCabal keyhive identity) re-keys.
* ''Boards resolve per relation''; the keyring joins delivery by scoped key and refuses different bytes under one name; one fleet-join door replaces `device-admit` and `persona admit`; add-a-face (A); the bare primary-path seal import goes; the mirror guard keys on posture.
* The staging harness founds through the one act inside the train, because the e2e set reads red from the first charter-byte change until the founding lands.

''R-WIRE, the per-Nexus wire key'' (waits on R-CHARTER).
* A per-Nexus wire key derived from the sealed custody root and the Nexus AID; one gate per (Nexus, route), so knock, pin, verdict and posture read per Nexus exactly.
* ''Noise IK, proof-first'': the gate emits nothing before a valid first message; the knock becomes the IK pre-message. It retires the `lar:challenge` frame.
* The floor announcement signs under the wire key, folded in here, so gate and announce never sign under two keys.
* One client dial per (hearth, Nexus), so a vessel walks at more than one hearth; until then a second walk hearth refuses aloud.
* The vessel key stops linking a walker's leaves across Nexuses on the wire (`two-maps` clause 5's residual), and the oracle pointer's vessel-key correlation goes with it (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/pronaos#/the-rung-ladder`).
* ''Per-dial transport keys on the sibling channel'': a leaf proves to a herm under a key fresh per dial, and the leaf-peer transcript binds that key in place of the device key the relay stamps, so a herm colluding with a revoked leaf reads no sibling's device key off its own gate (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/identity-slot-policy#/the-persona-group-secret`).
* ''Owed (a), the dialer's name rides the clear'': the dialer's ContactCard crosses in cleartext in `lar:auth` over plain `ws://` (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/auth-wire.ts`, `LarAuthMsg`) — a live THR_2 leak that Noise IK's encrypted first message cures, the dialer's static riding inside message one instead.
* ''The admit signature the build owes'': the canon-document list and gate key enter the signed bytes; `device-admit.ts` carries them outside the signature (`packages/lararium-node/src/commands/device-admit.ts:302-313`). The browser's first proof rides `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol` (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof`).
* ''Owed (b), the knock tests offline'': the knock path computes an HMAC of the public gate key (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/gate-knock.ts`, `knockSegment`); an observer holding candidate keys tests a sniffed knock offline, so not echoing the key protects only against an observer with no candidates.

''Behind the train, each waiting on R-CHARTER''.
* ''R-KAHU'': S1, a quorum whose seats live on different vessels, and L1, the realm root map from the co-signed `keptBy`. Needs only the seat shape.
* ''R-ENGINE, the offers'': the engine-offer lineage signs under the cadence threshold, adopted within a k-hop window along `prev`.
* ''R-EMBER'': a household's first ember stands on its second device, which needs the fleet-join door; the slot tree carries passphrase and paper pins today, and this slice adds the ember pin kind.

!!! 5 · ONE FACE ~ the recorded switch act (waits on the signer and the realm shapes)
* The slice builds `session-contract.mem` in its build order and `face-proof.mem`. A session wears the face; the vessel wears none. The switch acts on ONE session and closes and re-dials only that session's links under the incoming face's scoped proof.
* ''Working-layer law the build owes'': one working document per (face, wiki, slot kind); its address derives from a secret only that face's vessels hold. The working layer follows its wiki across recipe recomposition. A leaf with an admit and no seat writes nothing to face wikis until join completion. Vessel removal leaves the address standing; the gate and document membership refuse the removed vessel (operator ruling).
* A wiki names one face's composition over bags; sharing happens at the bag (operator ruling).
* A personal Handle never rides without its owning face; one current personal Handle per PersonaGroup; a collective quorum Handle stays separate.
* The compose hunk in `open-node-vessel.ts` belongs to this slice's owner; cabal runtime work stops at its module boundary.

!!! 6 · TWO HEARTHS ~ the fresh start, then the ceremony (waits on everything above)
* The fresh start by hand (#/fresh-start) on both machines, on the re-found build.
* The ceremony: both found their seeds through the Stable act and stand; both found the Nexus fresh through the Nexus founding act; each signs a contract the two seats countersign; both join one shared cabal realm beside others; each wears a face; an edit crosses both ways and reads back after a restart on each machine. Probes use synthetic names; no fixture forges a cast persona's key or Handle.

!!! The shore ~ the browser's five rungs, beside the paths
* ''The rungs'': 0, an admitted crossing; 1, a carrier the node holds arrives and renders; 2, a typed field edit reaches the node and survives its restart, read from a fresh browser profile; 3, one human, many faces; 4, two hearths. Rungs 0–2 give the one-machine witness the working-Lararium ruling asks for, and the node's own read decides each rung, never a screenshot or a connected socket.
* ''What blocks rungs 0–2'': rungs 0 and 1 pass live (`tools/browser-ci.sh`, the crossing and render walks); rung 2's leaf half saves and survives a reload of the same profile, while its node half waits on ONE FACE's working layer. The node owes a ''durability receipt'' on the authenticated session (`docUrl`, the stored heads, the holder's boot epoch, sent only after the holder commits those heads; no clock in the frame).
* Device capabilities get measured by `tools/capability-probe/` on real devices, never assumed.
* ''What blocks rung 3 onward'': rung 3 waits on ONE FACE. Rung 4 waits on HARBOR's household HTTPS, on the KAHU PLANE's grant for a foreign leaf, and on #/open's second question before a persona root enters a browser.
* The walks ride `tools/browser-ci.sh`, and build under `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/dreamdom#/the-ruling`: a frame carries absolute state, and a worker defines no global `window`.

!!! Off this road, by ruling
* ''Cloud carriers'': capability and carrier law, private sealed carriage, per-store lifecycle, causal wake and its measurement. Deferred until the operator can deploy; the reading of "delegated keys" (a narrow carriage grant, or a backup that can act) stays open with them.
* ''The Sensorium'': waits behind the working-Lararium milestone.
* ''Streams'' (`saqimtiaz/streams`, BSD-3-Clause): a deferred crossroads offering; it never rides the genesis boot set.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/fresh-start>>

!! The fresh start ~ by hand, reversible, never a command

NO pave or wipe command stands (operator-ruled): such a command carries too much risk. In early alpha a fresh
START still reads pono when it runs BY HAND: MOVE the old homes aside — rename them, never delete them, so
the move stays reversible — then found fresh on the re-found build. Valuable data lives in git, in the
private `claude-backup` repository, or on `D:`; a vessel home holds nothing that only it holds.

The homes to move: the ones the OLD build's resolvers name — the `~/.lares` home, the XDG data homes for
`lares` and `lararium`, and the state home — asked of the old build at the moment of moving, never
enumerated by hand. A founding refuses a store that holds an earlier shape (`OldShapeRefusal`, before any
route gets asked and before any write), which keeps an accidental founding off any home left unmoved. Other
Nexuses rejoin through presented admits; every old admit names an old AID and reads dead by construction.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/open>>

!! Open ~ what the operator still owns

# ''Seats, recovery and the user road.'' Single-seat Kahu acts, an operator's own herm, guardian count and succession, paper, the backup bundle, the catastrophe rite and the user road's bound stand open at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus">>.
# ''A root-holding browser leaf's backup slot.'' A WebCrypto non-extractable slot allows no paper backup in-browser; does "a backup at every tier" require such a leaf to hold a paper or passphrase slot too? Lean: yes for a leaf holding a persona root; a floor-only leaf holds no tree.
# ''A sealed receipt manifest for carriage.'' It would let a root restored from paper re-open its carriage after device loss — and would turn carriage toward the backup W rules it never becomes. The lenses split; re-opening W needs the operator's turned key.
# ''Carriage across leaves.'' A guest-held carry-claim proven at the carry door would let carriage follow a guest across leaves — a second relation and a second domain. Lean: not built.
# ''The Kahu's DNS domain and credential home.'' Name the domain and the store for its DNS credential.
# ''The fork-resolve act's shape.'' The seal record holds one predecessor.
# ''The re-seal proof.'' Name the causal record that maps old ciphertext to its replacement.

